# AI Agent Skill File: API Endpoint Documentation

## Overview

This skill file was created as part of my exploration of AI-ready documentation practices, 
prompted by participation in the Write the Docs book club reading *Docs as Tests & AI* by 
Manny Silva. As AI agents become increasingly integrated into documentation workflows, 
the ability to write structured guidance that machines can follow reliably is an emerging 
and valuable technical writing skill.

A skill.md file is a structured document that tells an AI agent how to complete a specific 
task — in this case, documenting a REST API endpoint. Unlike documentation written for 
humans, a skill file must be unambiguous, sequenced, and explicit about constraints, 
because AI agents cannot infer intent the way a human reader can.

## Design Decisions

**Why test-first?**  
The workflow requires testing the endpoint before writing anything. This guardrail exists 
because AI-generated documentation based on specs alone frequently contains inaccuracies. 
Requiring firsthand testing grounds the output in observed behavior rather than assumed behavior.

**Why separate required and optional parameters?**  
Developer experience research consistently shows that developers scan documentation 
rather than read it linearly. Separating required from optional parameters reduces 
cognitive load at the moment a developer is constructing a request.

**Why label inferred field definitions?**  
API documentation frequently contains undefined fields — official documentation simply 
doesn't define every key in a response object. Rather than leaving gaps or guessing 
silently, this skill requires the agent to flag inferred definitions explicitly, 
preserving transparency for the human reviewer.

**Why prohibit placeholder values in examples?**  
Placeholder values like "your-api-key" or "example-value" reduce the usefulness of 
code examples. Realistic values help developers recognize what a real response looks 
like and reduce the time from reading to implementation.

